Runtime: Memory Model

Purpose:
  Define the 4-tier memory hierarchy and TMP memory for AI working memory.
  Memory tiers govern what knowledge is loaded, retained, and promoted
  across task executions, optimizing token usage and preserving engineering
  truth at the appropriate persistence level.

Principles:
  Working memory is runtime-only and never persisted to .sdd/.
  TMP memory is transient for external analysis only.
  Memory promotion is evidence-driven, not assumed.
  AI remembers decisions, not conversations.
  Token budget is optimized by loading only relevant memory tier content.

MemoryTiers:
  LongTermKnowledge:
    definition: permanent engineering truth
    scope: cross-project, survives project deletion
    examples:
      - @best-practice.solid
      - @best-practice.kiss
      - @ddd.aggregate
      - @architecture.hexagonal
      - @security.password-hashing
    schema:
      id: canonical reference
      type: concept | pattern | skill
      status: established
      trust: proven
      evidence_count: integer
      projects_used: integer
      tasks_referenced: integer
    ttl: indefinite
    importance_rating: critical | high | normal | low | temporary
    persistence: permanent

  ProjectMemory:
    definition: project-specific knowledge
    scope: single project lifecycle
    examples:
      - @project.payment (PostgreSQL, modular-monolith, RabbitMQ, JWT)
      - @project.api-gateway
    schema:
      id: canonical reference
      project: project_id
      type: architecture | decision | constraint | skill
      status: active | deprecated
      trust: validated
      evidence:
        - task_id
        - decision_id
        - test_result
    ttl: project lifetime
    importance_rating: critical | high | normal | low | temporary
    persistence: project-scoped

  TaskMemory:
    definition: current task knowledge
    scope: active task execution only
    examples:
      - TASK-1023: implement wallet withdrawal
      - relevant: @payment.idempotency, @wallet.aggregate
      - decisions: @decision.atomic-balance-update
    schema:
      task_id: string
      goal: string
      relevant:
        - reference_id
      decisions:
        - decision_id
      status: active | completed
    ttl: task lifetime
    importance_rating: critical | high | normal | low | temporary
    persistence: task-scoped

  WorkingMemory:
    definition: current reasoning context
    scope: single task execution iteration
    examples:
      - file: wallet.go
      - problem: balance mutation
      - constraints: atomic, idempotent
      - candidate: transaction + aggregate lock
    schema:
      current:
        file: string
        problem: string
        constraints: list
      candidate: string
      observation: string
      insight: string
    ttl: task execution iteration
    importance_rating: critical | high | normal | low | temporary
    persistence: runtime-only, never persisted to .sdd/

   TmpMemory:
    definition: external material and temporary analysis
    scope: transient analysis, never part of project knowledge
    schema:
      source: external | scratch
      content: string
      analysis: string
    ttl: immediate (end of reasoning step)
    importance_rating: temporary
    persistence: runtime-only, never persisted

  RuntimeBridge:
    definition: Maps .sdd memory tiers to claude-mem physical stores
    source: thedotmack/claude-mem SessionStore.ts + analysis-comprehensive.md
    best_practices_ref: tmp/web-research/best-practices-findings.md#key-finding-7
    stores:
      SQLite:
        tier: TaskMemory + ProjectMemory metadata
        schema: 49 migrations, SHA-256 content_hash, FTS5 for full-text
        location: ~/.claude-mem.db (per-user)
        role: primary metadata store for session_id, content_session_id, observations
        dedup: SHA-256 content-hash, ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING

      ChromaDB:
        tier: LongTermKnowledge (semantic layer)
        schema: vector embeddings (text-embedding-3-small)
        location: Chroma server (default localhost:8000)
        role: semantic search with tier routing
        dedup: same as SQLite (content_hash foreign key)

      SHA-256_HashStore:
        tier: All tiers (dedup key)
        schema: content_hash as primary key
        role: cross-store dedup, integrity verification
        note: Prevents duplicate observations from multiple harness hooks

    TrustMarkers:
      definition: Provenance classification for all imported/external content
      best_practices_ref: tmp/web-research/best-practices-findings.md#key-finding-7
      source: MemTrust + Memory Seam models; Phase 149 upgrade (2026-09-07)
      markers:
        trustLevel:
          values: "external-import" | "internal-verified" | "runtime-generated"
          default: "runtime-generated" for agent observations
          rule: Every imported memory carries trustLevel marker
          rule: Downstream packaging code MUST read trustLevel before injection
        provenance:
          values: "script_confirmed" | "provider_untrusted" | "llm_asserted"
          rule: External API results are provider_untrusted until schema-validated
          rule: Local deterministic computations are script_confirmed
          rule: AI-generated content is llm_asserted
        readiness:
          values: "fresh" | "stale" | "degraded" | "not-run" | "unknown"
          rule: Provider results marked degraded if schema validation passes but
            freshness is uncertain
          rule: Fail-safe on unavailable provider: mark unknown, emit OBS17
        temporal_validity:
          definition: Bi-temporal supersession per knowledge unit (Graphiti pattern,
            Phase 149; source: r1-memory research round 3, 2026-09-07)
          fields:
            t_valid: "when the fact became true (event time)"
            t_invalid: "when superseded/invalidated (null = still valid)"
            supersedes: "reference-id of the invalidated knowledge unit"
          rule: New validated facts INVALIDATE (set t_invalid), never delete —
            .sdd/ immutability preserves supersession history
          rule: Contradiction detected → old unit t_invalid = new unit t_valid
          rule: Implicit conflicts (later observation invalidates without
            explicit negation — STALE benchmark pattern) MUST trigger
            consolidation review, not silent coexistence

    CryptoErasure:
      definition: Cryptographic erasure (crypto-shredding) for GDPR/right-to-be-forgotten
      best_practices_ref: tmp/web-research/best-practices-findings.md#key-finding-7
      source: MemTrust Layer 3 (Learning & Evolution)
      design:
        DUK_per_memory_unit: Each memory unit gets unique Data Unit Key
        key_hierarchy: DUK -> DEK (Data Encryption Key) -> MEK (Master Encryption Key)
        deletion_mechanism: Destroy DUK to render encrypted data computationally
          intractable (no need to scrub storage)
        key_storage: DUKs stored in TEE-protected key vault
        audit: Key destruction events are cryptographically chained

    PolicyAsCode:
      definition: Access control policies enforced at runtime
      best_practices_ref: tmp/web-research/best-practices-findings.md#key-finding-7
      source: MemTrust Governance Layer
      engine: Open Policy Agent (OPA) compiled to WebAssembly
      policy_binding: Policies hashed and bound to enclave measurement
      rule: Policy changes invalidate attestation (cannot be silently modified)
      attestation: Attestation-bound OIDC tokens for secure access
      rule: Tokens cryptographically bound to TLS channel + TEE session
      tamper_evidence: Audit logs cryptographically chained; head hash signed
        and anchored to immutable ledger (blockchain/transparency log)

    PersistenceMapping:
      LongTermKnowledge -> ChromaDB (semantic) + SQLite (metadata)
      ProjectMemory -> SQLite (primary) + ChromaDB (semantic index)
      TaskMemory -> SQLite (per-execution tables) + in-memory cache
      WorkingMemory -> runtime-only (never persisted)
      TmpMemory -> runtime-only (never persisted)

    RecallMechanism:
      tier_routing: filter -> ChromaDB semantic -> FTS5 fallback
      recency_gate: 90-day window (configurable via CLAUDE_MEM_MAX_RECALL_AGE)
      token_budget: progressive disclosure (metadata first ~100 tokens,
        instructions < 5000 tokens, resources on demand)
      sanitization: L0 sanitize step before injection (prompt-injection prevention)
      hot_cold: hot (referenced ≥50% recent) loaded by default, cold (not
        referenced 90 days) loaded on demand only
      fused_scoring:
        definition: Additive fused retrieval score replacing pure cascade
          (Phase 149; Mem0 v3 + Generative Agents pattern, r1 round 3)
        formula: score = w_sem * semantic_sim + w_kw * FTS5/BM25_match +
          w_ent * entity_match + w_rec * recency_decay + w_imp * importance
        defaults: w_sem 0.35, w_kw 0.20, w_ent 0.15, w_rec 0.15, w_imp 0.15
        rule: recency_decay applies INSIDE the 90-day gate (exponential,
          half-life 30 days) — recency ranks, never gates alone
        rule: t_invalid (superseded) units are excluded from recall
      principal_scoping:
        definition: Principal-Scoped Retrieval (VMG primitive 3, Phase 149)
        rule: Every recall is scoped by project/agent principal — no cross-
          principal bleed (Claude Projects isolation pattern)
        rule: Recall requests carry requesting principal id; store filters

    WritePaths:
      definition: Dual write-path policy (LangMem pattern, Phase 149)
      hot_path:
        use: critical + importance-rated knowledge (decisions, security)
        behavior: explicit, immediate, auditable write with full provenance
        rule: RT-MM-05 decisions are ALWAYS hot-path
      background_path:
        use: observations, insights, everything else
        behavior: deferred consolidation (see Consolidation triggers)
        rule: tool outputs and raw transcripts NEVER enter hot path

    Rollbackability:
      definition: VMG primitive 5 — version chains on knowledge units
        (MemCube pattern, Phase 149)
      mechanism: every .sdd knowledge edit is git-versioned (chain = git
        history of the unit); supersession adds t_valid/t_invalid links
      rule: any knowledge unit's full evolution is reconstructable from
        git log + supersedes chain — no destructive edits exist

    Consolidation:
      ratio: 100 runtime events -> ~3 decisions + 1 new skill
      source: .sdd/runtime/memory-consolidation.sdd
      hot_classification: referenced in ≥50% of recent tasks in last 14 days
      cold_classification: not referenced in 90 days
      storage: consolidated output stored to ProjectMemory / LongTermKnowledge
      triggers:
        definition: Sleep-time consolidation triggers (Letta/OpenAI pattern,
          Phase 149, r1 round 3)
        events:
          - end_of_task (existing RT-MM-12 baseline)
          - step_count_threshold (N steps since last consolidation, default 25)
          - context_compaction (compaction event fires consolidation)
          - session_idle (idle gap detected)
        rule: consolidation runs OFF the user's latency clock (background pass)
        rule: background pass MAY use a stronger model than the live agent
          (latency-unconstrained — Letta sleep-time guidance)

MemoryPromotionPipeline:
  stages:
    WORKING_MEMORY:
      description: active reasoning context
      next: OBSERVATION

    OBSERVATION:
      description: noticed pattern or fact without evidence
      next: INSIGHT

    INSIGHT:
      description: hypothesis or connection with some evidence
      next: CANDIDATE

    CANDIDATE:
      description: proposed knowledge awaiting validation
      ttl: 30 days (auto-expires to ARCHIVE if unvalidated)
      next: VALIDATED_KNOWLEDGE

    VALIDATED_KNOWLEDGE:
      description: confirmed by evidence, ready for promotion
      next: LONG_TERM_KNOWLEDGE

    LONG_TERM_KNOWLEDGE:
      description: permanent engineering truth
      persistence: permanent
      skill_crystallization:
        definition: procedural memory (skills) as first-class promotion target
          (Phase 149; Letta Skill Learning / MemOS crystallized skills pattern)
        rule: repeated task patterns in VALIDATED_KNOWLEDGE route to a skills
          tier with evidence-driven promotion (TASK/DECISION/PRODUCTION)
        rule: crystallized skills land in .sdd/skills/{domain}/ with full
          provenance per import rules

  promotion_rules:
    - promotion requires evidence: CODE, TEST, TASK, DECISION, PROJECT, PRODUCTION, EXTERNAL, HUMAN
    - candidate without validation after 30 days is archived
    - validated knowledge may be promoted to long-term after project verification
    - project-specific knowledge does not auto-promote to long-term
    - trust hierarchy: explicit project constraints > decisions > validated project skills > established org knowledge > general best practices > AI suggestions

MemoryImportance:
  critical:
    description: security decisions, data integrity, architectural invariants
    examples:
      - @constitution.security
      - @constitution.data-integrity
    always_load: true

  high:
    description: core domain decisions, primary patterns
    examples:
      - @decision.rabbitmq
      - @payment.idempotency

  normal:
    description: supporting knowledge, secondary patterns
    examples:
      - @ddd.specification
      - @architecture.repository

  low:
    description: rarely used, historical, optional
    examples:
      - @legacy.payment-v1
    default_context: false

  temporary:
    description: debugging hypothesis, migration scratch, transient analysis
    examples:
      - @insight.current-migration
    ttl: task-lifetime or explicit expires

Navigation:
  Runtime: @INDEX.sdd
  Context: @context.sdd
  Context Builder: @context-builder.sdd
  Context Policy: @context-policy.sdd
  Context Manifest: @context-manifest.sdd
  Memory Consolidation: @memory-consolidation.sdd
  Execution: @execution.sdd
  Agent: @../agent/INDEX.sdd

Rules:
  [RT-MM-01] Working memory is runtime-only and MUST NOT be persisted to .sdd/.
  [RT-MM-02] TMP memory is transient for external analysis only and MUST NOT be persisted.
  [RT-MM-03] Long-term knowledge survives project deletion; project memory does not.
  [RT-MM-04] Memory promotion is evidence-driven; candidates without validation expire after 30 days.
  [RT-MM-05] AI MUST remember decisions, not conversations.
  [RT-MM-06] Trust hierarchy: explicit project constraints > decisions > validated project skills > established org knowledge > general best practices > AI suggestions.
  [RT-MM-07] Memory importance rating determines default context inclusion.
  [RT-MM-08] Critical importance knowledge MUST be always loaded.
  [RT-MM-09] Low importance knowledge MUST NOT be in default context; loaded on demand only.
  [RT-MM-10] Temporary importance knowledge MUST have explicit TTL or task-lifetime bound.
  [RT-MM-11] Project memory does not auto-promote to long-term knowledge.
  [RT-MM-12] Memory consolidation occurs at end of each task execution.
  [RT-MM-13] Agent MUST NOT add signatures or sign-offs to memory outputs.
  [RT-MM-14] Agent MUST NOT use emojis, icons, or decorative symbols in memory output headers or titles.
  [RT-MM-15] Superseded knowledge units (t_invalid set) MUST be excluded from
    recall and NEVER deleted — supersession is invalidation, not removal.
  [RT-MM-16] Hot-path writes are reserved for decisions and critical/importance-
    rated knowledge; all else takes the background consolidation path.
  [RT-MM-17] Recall requests MUST carry a principal (project/agent) scope;
    cross-principal retrieval is a security violation.
  [RT-MM-18] Consolidation background passes MAY use a stronger model than
    the live agent; live-path latency MUST NOT pay for memory housekeeping.

State: +
